#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124167 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "2247dc40-0c71-596c-9da4-a3bffd85e106" :question/math-academy-id "e-3152" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n$f_x(x,y) = 6xy+axy^3-5, \\qquad f_y(x,y) = 3x^2+bx^2y^2, \\qquad f_y(2,-1) = -24$." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{xy}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(f_{x}(x, y)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy + axy^{3} - 5) \\\\\n&= 6x + 3axy^{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= 6x + 3axy^{2} \\\\\nf_{yx}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(f_{y}(x, y)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} + bx^{2}y^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6x + 2bxy^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. So, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{xy}(x, y) &= f_{yx}(x, y) \\\\\n6x + 3axy^{2} &= 6x + 2bxy^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy^2$, we see that $3a=2b$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf_y(2,-1)=-24\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{y}(2,-1) &= -24 \\\\\n3(2)^{2} + b(2)^{2}(-1)^{2} &= -24 \\\\\n12 + 4b &= -24 \\\\\n4b &= -36 \\\\\nb &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= 2b \\\\\n3a &= 2(-9) \\\\\n3a &= -18 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-6$ and $b=-9$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124168 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions of Partial Derivatives at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d45926a5-0126-50b4-bdc7-a9f686408986" :question/math-academy-id "e-3153" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y,z)$. Given that all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, and that\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z\\partial x} = -e^xy\\sin{(yz)}$,\n\nevaluate the following expression at the point $(1,\\pi,1)$:\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial z\\partial x\\partial z}+ \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial x \\partial z \\partial y}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} &= \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂z∂x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(-e^{x}y\\sin (yz)) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}y^{2}\\cos (yz)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-e^{x}y\\sin (yz)) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\sin (yz) - e^{x}yz\\cos (yz) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}(\\sin (yz) + yz\\cos (yz))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,\\pi,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & _{(1, π, 1)} &= -e^{(1)}(π)^{2}\\cos (π \\cdot 1) = eπ^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} & _{(1, π, 1)}|=-e^{(1)}(\\sin (π \\cdot 1) + (π)(1)\\cos (π \\cdot 1)) = eπ\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} \\mid _{(1, π, 1)} + \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} \\mid _{(1, π, 1)} &= eπ^{2} + eπ = eπ(π + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124169 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/id #uuid "d62e8502-b3c5-5923-8ab3-ef988507cd2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3154" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$?\n\n1. $f_x(x,y)=x+2y$ and $f_y(x,y)=2y-x$\n2. $f_x(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$ and $f_y(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$\n3. $f_x(x,y)=-\\sin{x}\\sin{y}$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos{x}\\cos{y}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is,\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y)\n$$\n\nLet's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + 2y) = 2,; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y - x) =-1$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) \\neq f_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could *not* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n- For the second pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5(x + y)^{4}) = 20(x + y)^{3},; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5(x + y)^{4}) = 20(x + y)^{3}$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) = f_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n- For the third pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sin x\\sin y) =-\\sin x\\cos y,; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\cos x\\cos y) =-\\sin x\\cos y$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa3dd6e-173c-5721-9fa0-45817c8b033e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122507} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bb5e40-9239-5f43-8566-fb3df3a1adb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122508} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0cea74-0e4e-542f-a13e-9d003ce9c7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122509} {:answer/id #uuid "fa499af7-d184-521c-b365-1980cbc53e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122510} {:answer/id #uuid "2e41c9ee-2f4c-5ea5-b49e-b40fb62b7c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7bb5e40-9239-5f43-8566-fb3df3a1adb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122508} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cfd987-276f-53e3-93d3-a4fa80ef61b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122506}] :question/id #uuid "574e3825-54df-5b43-b74f-6544e0543982" :question/math-academy-id "q-36518" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. $f_{x}(x, y) = x + 4y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = 3x - y$\n2. $f_{x}(x, y) = 2x$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = 0$\n3. $f_{x}(x, y) = 2x + \\cos x\\cos y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = e^{y} - \\sin x\\sin y$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "920ecefb-95f1-561a-813b-6b8f75581ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123635} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c4d50f-d5e6-512a-8c4f-42ea78e52a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123636} {:answer/id #uuid "abc1e336-a312-51d5-b5aa-35be15b4cb9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123637} {:answer/id #uuid "bd078c21-9e89-58c2-b161-6274f9988ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123638} {:answer/id #uuid "35983e6e-a5be-5cba-9cbf-d34ea2b13e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "920ecefb-95f1-561a-813b-6b8f75581ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123635} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5bf7e96-15a3-56ee-abdf-35bed217e0f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123634}] :question/id #uuid "3caf1655-4ce4-5a5f-995b-a06e33069b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65127" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. $f_{x}(x, y) = x^{3}y - x$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = xy^{3} - y$\n2. $f_{x}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2x$\n3. $f_{x}(x, y) = \\sqrt{x - 1}$ and $f_{y}(x, y) =-3$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801d1a61-bd92-5d33-ada1-f551f36d0def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 6$, $b =-8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123641} {:answer/id #uuid "af333e6d-e5ed-5170-a06d-f99be36c1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 6$, $b = 8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123642} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c95bc3-dd32-51f2-ae51-24431ba5b203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a =-3$, $b = 8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123643} {:answer/id #uuid "031335b2-8e98-57ff-9c19-ff94de326341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 3$, $b =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123644} {:answer/id #uuid "46171297-2705-59ac-8858-76e9c583e649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a =-2$, $b = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801d1a61-bd92-5d33-ada1-f551f36d0def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 6$, $b =-8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123641} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75542c6-f1f7-5ede-93be-db2b9103738d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123640}] :question/id #uuid "a266a271-7798-5a7e-9603-9dd916caddcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-65136" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n\n$f_{x}(x, y) = ax^{2}y - 4y^{2} + 3, f_{y}(x, y) = 2x^{3} + bxy - 8$."}]}
